Factory Method Design Pattern — 5-minute presentation
Interview context: Java 21 · Senior Backend Engineer · Design patterns and architecture
===============================================================================================================================================
0:00–0:40 — Introduction and intent

      The Factory Method is a creational design pattern defined by the Gang of Four, or GoF.
      
      Its primary intent is :
              to define a method for creating an object in a base Creator class, 
              while allowing subclasses to decide which concrete Product implementation should be instantiated.
      
      The key idea is to separate:
            the decision about which object to create 
            from the business workflow that uses that object.
      
      I would consider this pattern: 
            when a backend system supports multiple implementations of a capability, 
            and the object-creation logic varies across different workflows, 
              while the overall business process remains largely the same.
      
      The pattern promotes:
            programming to an abstraction,   
            reduces direct coupling to concrete implementations, 
            and provides an extension point through polymorphism.


